_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 15
---
order: 15
---
short_label: VI.15
---
statement: In equal triangles which have one angle equal to one angle the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional; and those triangles which have one angle equal to one angle, and in which the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional, are equal.
---
proof:

Let *ABC* and *ADE* be equal triangles having one angle equal to one angle, namely the angle *BAC* equal to the angle *DAE.*

I say that in the triangles *ABC* and *ADE* the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional, that is to say, that *CA* is to *AD* as *EA* is to *AB.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="270" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.15 diagram" src="propVI15.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "VI.15",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;140,110",
            "B;point;free;80,30",
            "C;point;free;30,200",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "D;point;lineSlider;A,C,200,50",
            "E';point;extend;B,A,B,A;0;0",
            "E;point;proportion;A,D,A,C,A,B,A,E'",
            "ADE;polygon;triangle;A,D,E;0;0;black;random",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Place them so that *CA* is in a straight line with *AD.* Therefore *EA* is also in a straight line with *AB.* [!just I.14]

Join *BD.*

Since then the triangle *ABC* equals the triangle *ADE,* and *ABD* is another triangle, therefore the triangle *ABC* is to the triangle *ABD* as the triangle *ADE* is to the triangle *ABD.* [!just V.7]

But *ABC* is to *ABD* as *AC* is to *AD,* and *ADE* is to *ABD* as *AE* is to *AB.* [!just VI.1]

Therefore also *AC* is to *AD* as *AE* is to *AB.* [!just V.11]

Therefore in the triangles *ABC* and *ADE* the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional.

---

Next, let the sides of the triangles *ABC* and *ADE* be reciprocally proportional, that is to say, let *AE* be to *AB* as *CA* is to *AD.*

I say that the triangle *ABC* equals the triangle *ADE.*

If *BD* is again joined, since *AC* is to *AD* as *AE* is to *AB,* while *AC* is to *AD* as the triangle *ABC* is to the triangle *ABD,* and *AE* is to *AB* as the triangle *ADE* is to the triangle *ABD,* therefore the triangle *ABC* is to the triangle *ABD* as the triangle *ADE* is to the triangle *ABD.* [!just VI.1; V.11]

Therefore each of the triangles *ABC* and *ADE* has the same ratio to *ABD.*

Therefore the triangle *ABC* equals the triangle *ADE.* [!just V.9]

Therefore, *in equal triangles which have one angle equal to one angle the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional; and those triangles which have one angle equal to one angle, and in which the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional, are equal.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is used in the proof of proposition @VI.19.
